ited Nations System Code of Conduct UNTERM Visualizations and machine readability Home Language careers Outreach Other partnerships Other partnerships Remote practicum agreements Apart from the MoU network and the associated membership agreements, partner universities may also be invited to sign a remote practicum agreement with the United Nations. Such agreements offer remote practical experience to promising university students studying translation or interpretation. Universities currently&nbsp;under a remote practicum agreement: Universidad EAN (Colombia) University of Lomé (Togo) UCLouvain (Belgium) Universities previously under a remote practicum agreement: Beijing Foreign Studies University (China)&nbsp; Shanghai International Studies University (China)&nbsp; University of the Republic (Uruguay) Ain Shams University (Egypt) Moscow State Linguistic University (Russian Federation) National University of Cordoba (Argentina) The University of the West Indies (Jamaica) University of Belgrano (Argentina) Women’s University of the Sacred Heart (Peru) The Pan-African Masters Consortium in Interpretation and Translation Purpose and background The Pan-African Masters Consortium in Interpretation and Translation (PAMCIT) aimed to provide international, regional and subregional organizations operating on the African continent with the capacity to serve intergovernmental processes in the long run by supporting succession planning. PAMCIT was initially set up in 2015 with the aim of creating a network of centres of excellence in Africa to produce qualified, skilled and trained translators and interpreters for Africa and beyond. To this end, the programme was established to train students as nationals of African states, as well as to ensure continuity by training interpretation and translation trainers. PAMCIT was funded mainly by the European Union with financial, technical and training assistance also from United Nations at Nairobi (UNON). The first phase of PAMCIT concluded on 31 December 2019. The second phase started in 2023 under the coordination of the ECOWAS Bank for Investment and Development, and since March 2025, the ECOWAS Commission.&nbsp; Members The first phase included five universities: Gaston Berger University (Senegal) Universidade Pedagógica de Moçambique (Mozambique) University of Buea (Cameroon) University of Ghana (Ghana) University of Nairobi (Kenya) &nbsp; &nbsp; MoU network Other partnerships St. Jerome Translation Contest Donate facebook twitter youtube flickr Footer A-Z Site Index Contact Copyright FAQ Fraud Alert Privacy Notice Terms of Use",
